.TH "ST\-WL\-PASTE" "1" "January 2020" "secure-terminal" "secure-terminal Manual"
.SH "NAME"
\fBst\-wl\-paste\fR \- wl\-paste that sanitizes the clipboard on read
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBst\-wl\-paste\fR [\fIWL\-PASTE\-OPTION\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
st\-wl\-paste is a drop\-in replacement for \fBwl\-paste\fR(1) (Wayland) that filters the clipboard text through \fBsclip\fR(1) as it is read\. Every argument is forwarded verbatim to \fBwl\-paste\fR, so native options such as \fB\-\-primary\fR or \fB\-\-no\-newline\fR work unchanged\.
.P
By default the strip is strictest (printable ASCII only)\. Set the environment variable \fBSCLIP_MODE=unicode\fR to keep printable non\-ASCII instead\.
.P
Compose it like the original, sanitizing one or both sides:
.IP "" 4
.nf
st\-wl\-paste | st\-wl\-copy
st\-wl\-paste | wl\-copy
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
\fBSCLIP_MODE\fR
When set to \fBunicode\fR, keep printable non\-ASCII (passes \fB\-\-unicode\fR to \fBsclip\fR)\. Any other value (or unset) is the default ASCII\-only strip\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The exit status of the underlying \fBwl\-paste\fR | \fBsclip\fR pipeline; 127 if \fBwl\-paste\fR is not installed\.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBst\-wl\-copy\fR(1), \fBst\-xclip\fR(1), \fBsclip\fR(1), \fBwl\-paste\fR(1)
